WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.884
An object is valid only during its lifetime.

2
00:00:02.884 --> 00:00:10.815
That lifetime may be tied to automatic storage in a scope, dynamic allocation, container ownership, static storage, a resource manager, or a

3
00:00:10.815 --> 00:00:12.257
higher-level object graph.

4
00:00:12.257 --> 00:00:18.025
Once the lifetime ends, old access paths to that object no longer have a valid target.

5
00:00:18.025 --> 00:00:20.188
Ownership answers the question of responsibility.

6
00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:26.316
Who keeps the object alive, who may transfer it, and who destroys it when work is complete?

7
00:00:26.316 --> 00:00:33.887
A program is easier to review when ownership has one clear answer or a deliberately shared answer with a documented policy.

8
00:00:33.887 --> 00:00:35.689
Borrowing answers a different question.

9
00:00:35.689 --> 00:00:39.655
Who may temporarily observe or use an object without owning it?

10
00:00:39.655 --> 00:00:44.702
Borrowed access is useful and common, but it must remain shorter than the owner.

11
00:00:44.702 --> 00:00:51.191
A borrowed pointer, reference, iterator, span, or string view should not silently become a long-lived object dependency.

12
00:00:51.191 --> 00:00:54.435
Scope exits, destruction, movement, and temporaries all affect lifetime.

13
00:00:54.435 --> 00:00:57.319
A local object ends when its scope ends.

14
00:00:57.319 --> 00:01:02.366
A moved-from object remains alive but may no longer contain the expected value.

15
00:01:02.366 --> 00:01:05.971
A temporary may disappear sooner than a stored view expects.

16
00:01:05.971 --> 00:01:10.658
These rules matter because code often stores access paths separately from the owner.

17
00:01:10.658 --> 00:01:14.984
Lifetime safety starts by making ownership and borrowing visible in code structure.

18
00:01:14.984 --> 00:01:20.391
Values, RAII members, smart pointers, spans, references, callbacks, and handles should each communicate their role.

19
00:01:20.391 --> 00:01:26.880
When that meaning is unclear, reviewers should ask whether the object will still be alive at every use.
